Change data capture.

Incremental loading needs to know what changed in the source. Change data capture — C-D-C — is the set of techniques for identifying and capturing the inserts, updates, and deletes a source made, so you move only the delta, not the whole dataset. CDC is what makes efficient, near-real-time loading possible.

Let's walk the methods, from weakest to strongest. First, timestamp, or high-water. The source rows carry a last-modified column, and you grab everything changed since the last run. It's simple and cheap — but it misses deletes, and it can miss updates that don't touch the timestamp. Second, trigger-based. Database triggers write every change to a change table. This is accurate, but it adds overhead on the source, because every write now does extra work. Third, log-based CDC. Here you read the source database's transaction log — the redo log, or binlog. It captures every insert, update, and delete, with minimal source impact, because nothing extra runs on the operational system. This is the gold standard. And fourth, snapshot diff. You compare a fresh full snapshot to the last one. It catches everything, including deletes — but it's expensive, because you extract the whole source just to find the changes.

Why does CDC matter so much? Because re-extracting an entire busy source every run simply doesn't scale — and it hammers the operational system. CDC replaces "pull everything" with "pull only the changes." And that does three things. It feeds incremental loads, from section five — the delta is exactly what an incremental load writes. It drives the SCD-2 merge, from module six — a captured change to a dimension row is what triggers expire-and-insert. And it enables near-real-time loading — small, frequent deltas, instead of one giant nightly batch. So: CDC captures a source's inserts, updates, and deletes, so you load only the delta. Log-based CDC is the gold standard — complete and low-impact — and it's what powers incremental loads and SCD-2 merges at scale.
